const lessonDetails={
 plants:{heading:'A tiny seed, a big beginning',body:['Amina plants a bean seed in soft, damp soil. She places it by a sunny window and checks it each morning. After a few days, a small root pushes down while a green shoot reaches toward the light.','Plants need light, water, air and nutrients. Roots take in water from the soil. Leaves use sunlight to help the plant make food. As the stem grows taller, the young seedling can support more leaves.'],think:'What might happen to a seedling kept in a dark cupboard?',activity:'Observe a plant near your home or school. Draw its roots, stem and leaves, and note one change you see this week.',question:'Which plant part takes in most of the water from the soil?',options:['The roots','The flower petals','The fruit'],answer:'a',correct:'That’s right. Roots absorb water and help anchor the plant in the soil.',hint:'Think about the part that grows beneath the soil.'},
 fractions:{heading:'Sharing fruit fairly',body:['Musa brings an orange to share with four friends. He divides it into four equal pieces. Each piece is one quarter, written as 1/4. The number below the line tells how many equal pieces make one whole.','If Musa shares another orange in the same way, four quarters make one whole orange: 4/4 = 1. Equal-sized parts matter. If the pieces are different sizes, they are not fair fractions of the same whole.'],think:'If three of four equal orange pieces are eaten, what fraction is left?',activity:'Draw a circle for a chapati. Divide it into eight equal pieces and shade three. Write the shaded fraction.',question:'What does the 4 mean in the fraction 3/4?',options:['There are four equal parts in the whole','Three parts are left over','The whole has four different shapes'],answer:'a',correct:'Exactly. The denominator 4 tells us the whole is divided into four equal parts.',hint:'In a fraction, the bottom number describes the equal parts in the whole.'},
 stories:{heading:'The basket by the doorway',body:['On Saturday morning, Zuri visits her grandmother. A woven basket sits beside the door, filled with seed packets and a little hand trowel. Grandmother explains that she is preparing a garden for the rainy season.','Together they plant spinach near the kitchen and beans beside a sturdy fence. Zuri labels each row with a drawing. Before she leaves, Grandmother says, “Small care, given often, helps good things grow.” Zuri carries the words home like a story of her own.'],think:'Which detail shows that Zuri and her grandmother planned the garden together?',activity:'Retell the story in three parts: what happened first, what happened next, and how it ended.',question:'Why do Zuri and her grandmother label each garden row?',options:['So they can remember what is planted there','To make the seeds grow overnight','To keep the rain away'],answer:'a',correct:'Yes. The labels help them remember which seeds are growing in each row.',hint:'Look for the detail that helps them identify the spinach and bean rows.'},
 water:{heading:'Water’s journey through the sky',body:['After rain, the sun warms puddles, rivers and the ocean. Some water changes into an invisible gas called water vapour and rises into the air. This change is evaporation.','Higher in the cooler air, the vapour cools and forms tiny drops in clouds. This is condensation. When the drops join and become heavy, they fall as rain. Water then flows across the land or into the soil, and the cycle begins again.'],think:'Where might rainwater go after it lands on a hillside?',activity:'Draw arrows to show water moving from the ocean to clouds, falling as rain, and flowing back toward a river.',question:'What is it called when water vapour cools and forms cloud droplets?',options:['Condensation','Evaporation','Collection'],answer:'a',correct:'Correct. Condensation happens when water vapour cools into tiny liquid droplets.',hint:'The vapour has already risen. Think about what happens as it cools.'},
 shapes:{heading:'Geometry on the way to school',body:['On the walk to school, Leila spots shapes all around her. A road sign has three straight sides. A window has four sides and four square corners. The classroom clock is round, with no corners at all.','A triangle has three sides. A rectangle has four sides and four right angles. A square is a special rectangle: all four sides are the same length. We can describe shapes by counting their sides and comparing their angles.'],think:'Can you find one shape at home that has four equal sides?',activity:'Look around your room. List one triangle, one rectangle and one circle, then describe how you recognised each one.',question:'How many sides does a triangle always have?',options:['Three','Four','Five'],answer:'a',correct:'That’s right. A triangle has three straight sides and three corners.',hint:'The word “tri” gives us a clue. Count the sides of the road sign in the story.'},
 community:{heading:'A team that listens',body:['The class is making a garden sign. Each learner has a different idea. Before choosing a design, Nia asks everyone to explain what matters to them. She listens without interrupting and repeats the ideas so the group can check she understood.','The group combines a large title, bright colours and clear pictures. They share the jobs fairly, ask for help when needed and thank one another for their work. Listening helps the team make a plan that includes everyone.'],think:'How did Nia show the team that she was listening?',activity:'With a partner, plan a poster together. Take turns sharing one idea, then choose one detail from each person’s suggestion.',question:'What helped the learners make a plan that included everyone?',options:['Listening and combining ideas','Choosing the first idea without discussion','Asking one person to do every job'],answer:'a',correct:'Exactly. Listening and combining ideas helped each learner contribute.',hint:'Think about what Nia did before the group chose a design.'}
 ,'yoruba-garden':{heading:'Jẹ́ ká tọ́jú ọgbà wa',body:['Adé rí i pé ewéko kékeré kan wà nínú ọgbà ilé ẹ̀kọ́. Ó fún un ní omi díẹ̀, ó sì yẹ̀wò ilẹ̀ rẹ̀. Gbòǹgbò ń mú omi láti inú ilẹ̀, nígbà tí ewé ń lo ìmọ́lẹ̀ oòrùn láti ran ewéko lọ́wọ́ láti ṣe oúnjẹ.','Ewéko nílò omi, afẹ́fẹ́, oúnjẹ inú ilẹ̀ àti ìmọ́lẹ̀ oòrùn. A lè dáàbò bo ọgbà nípa fífún ewéko ní omi, yíyọ èpò kúrò, àti ṣíṣọ́ ilẹ̀ mọ́.'],think:'Kí ló lè ṣẹlẹ̀ sí ewéko tí kò bá rí omi fún ọ̀pọ̀ ọjọ́?',activity:'Wo ewéko kan nítòsí rẹ. Ya àwòrán rẹ̀, kí o sì kọ ohun kan tí o ṣe láti tọ́jú rẹ̀.',question:'Apá wo ni ewéko ń lo láti fa omi láti inú ilẹ̀?',options:['Gbòǹgbò','Òdòdó','Èso'],answer:'a',correct:'Ó tọ́. Gbòǹgbò ń fa omi láti inú ilẹ̀.',hint:'Ronú nípa apá ewéko tó wà nínú ilẹ̀.'},
 'swahili-water':{heading:'Maji husafiri katika mzunguko',body:['Jua linapopasha maji ya bahari, mito na madimbwi, baadhi ya maji hubadilika kuwa mvuke na kupanda angani. Mabadiliko haya huitwa uvukizaji.','Mvuke unapopoa angani, hutengeneza matone madogo katika mawingu. Hii huitwa mgandamizo. Matone yanapokuwa mazito, huanguka kama mvua. Maji hutiririka ardhini au kuingia kwenye udongo, na safari huanza tena.'],think:'Maji ya mvua yanaweza kwenda wapi baada ya kufika ardhini?',activity:'Chora mishale inayoonyesha maji yakitoka baharini, yakipanda mawinguni, yakinyesha kama mvua na kurudi mtoni.',question:'Maji ya mvua yanapopoa na kuwa matone ya mawingu, mabadiliko hayo huitwaje?',options:['Mgandamizo','Uvukizaji','Kuyeyuka'],answer:'a',correct:'Sahihi. Mgandamizo hutengeneza matone ya maji mawinguni wakati mvuke unapopoa.',hint:'Mvuke tayari umepanda. Fikiria kinachotokea unapopoa.'},
 'french-seed':{heading:'Du grain à la jeune plante',body:['Lina plante une graine de haricot dans un peu de terre humide. Elle place le pot près d’une fenêtre ensoleillée et l’arrose avec soin. Quelques jours plus tard, une petite racine pousse dans la terre et une tige verte se dirige vers la lumière.','Une plante a besoin d’eau, d’air, de nutriments et de lumière. Les racines absorbent l’eau. Les feuilles utilisent la lumière du soleil pour aider la plante à fabriquer sa nourriture. Avec des soins réguliers, la jeune plante grandit.'],think:'Que pourrait-il arriver à la jeune plante si elle restait dans un placard sombre?',activity:'Observe une plante près de chez toi. Dessine ses racines, sa tige et ses feuilles, puis note un changement que tu remarques.',question:'Quelle partie de la plante absorbe surtout l’eau du sol?',options:['Les racines','Les pétales','Le fruit'],answer:'a',correct:'Exactement. Les racines absorbent l’eau du sol et aident à ancrer la plante.',hint:'Pense à la partie qui pousse sous la terre.'}
};
